-- bisect on 'src/main.rs': search sub-tokens
   'src/main.rs' -> 0 hits
   'src/main rs' -> 0 hits
   'src main.rs' -> 1 hits
   'src/main.rs' -> 0 hits
   LIKE whole-string present: 1

-- zero-result discriminator: 'zzz-no-such-text-zzz' -> 0 hits; 'zzz/no-such/text' -> 0 hits

The bisect is the mechanism: adding a separator ONLY at the special character's
position (`src main.rs`) finds the row, while both the fused form and the
re-spaced form that does not respect the token boundary still answer 0. The
fix substitutes a space for every special character, which is what upstream
does (`_FTS5_SPECIAL_RE.sub(" ", sanitized)`, hermes_state_search.py:837).

NOTES
-----
* The brief described this matrix as "19/31 MISS". The stale file it replaced
  contained 22 MISS rows, and a fresh-store reproduction of the same revision
  answers 21/31 MISS here. The stale run was driven against a store other legs
  had already filled: its LIKE column reads 2 for every string, and its `search`
  column hits the probe's limit of 50 for `ok!` and `$5` (the pre-fix query
  `ok`/`5` matching many rows). All numbers in this file come from a fresh store
  with one row per string, so they are mutually comparable; the three strings
  that differ from the stale file are `note (see appendix)` (MISS -> ok),
  `ok!` (50 -> 1) and `$5` (50 -> 2).
* The pre-fix run of the SAME revision was reproduced twice — once with the
  binary built from the then-HEAD working tree, once from the scratch build of
  1093dfd above — and both answered the identical 21 MISS rows.
* Criterion 2 (the zero-hit LIKE fallback must be a genuine fallback, not a
  replacement for FTS5) is evidenced separately in zero-hit-fallback.log.

CRITERION 1 — the sanitizer's literal output, before and after
--------------------------------------------------------------
Measured with a throwaway print test appended to the sanitizer's own module in
the SCRATCH tree (the repo tree is never touched for this), then run as:

    CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/hrs-df105/prefix-target cargo test --lib \
      print_sanitized_output -- --nocapture

POST-FIX (stock_sqlite.rs copied from HEAD 6295059):

    SANITIZED "src/main.rs" -> "src \"main.rs\""  tokens=["src", "\"main.rs\""]
    SANITIZED "cost=5.50" -> "cost \"5.50\""  tokens=["cost", "\"5.50\""]
    SANITIZED "a,b;c" -> "a b c"  tokens=["a", "b", "c"]
    SANITIZED "path C:\\Users\\kara" -> "path C  Users kara"  tokens=["path", "C", "Users", "kara"]

'src/main.rs' yields the two tokens 'src' and 'main.rs'; 'cost=5.50' yields
'cost' and '5.50'. Step 5 then quotes the dotted token (\"main.rs\", \"5.50\"),
which is the MATCH-syntax spelling of the literal the row holds.

PRE-FIX (revision 1093dfd), the same probe:

    SANITIZED "src/main.rs" -> "\"srcmain.rs\""  tokens=["\"srcmain.rs\""]
    SANITIZED "cost=5.50" -> "\"cost5.50\""  tokens=["\"cost5.50\""]
    SANITIZED "a,b;c" -> "abc"  tokens=["abc"]
    SANITIZED "path C:\\Users\\kara" -> "path CUserskara"  tokens=["path", "CUserskara"]

Each special character simply vanished, fusing the words on either side into one
token no row contains — which step 5 then quoted as a phrase, so the query could
only answer zero. Substituting a space is the whole fix.
